// Lesson 4.1 — The Enemy: Patrol
// Unit 4 opens. EnemyController.cs already exists in the project as a patrol
// script — this lesson builds the patrol half of it.
//
// GROUND TRUTH (read from the teacher's project):
//   Assets/Scripts/EnemyController.cs — the built patrol:
//       float speed = 2;
//       int patrolNumber = 0;
//       [SerializeField] private List<Transform> patrolPoints = new List<Transform>();
//       Vector3 targetPosition;
//       Start():  targetPosition = patrolPoints[patrolNumber].position;
//       Update():
//           if (Vector3.Distance(transform.position, patrolPoints[patrolNumber].position) < .1)
//           { patrolNumber += 1; if (patrolNumber == patrolPoints.Count) patrolNumber = 0; }
//           targetPosition = patrolPoints[patrolNumber].position;
//           float step = speed * Time.deltaTime;
//           transform.position = Vector3.MoveTowards(transform.position, targetPosition, step);
//   Assets/Prefabs/MobileEnemy.prefab
//       root  tag Enemy, CapsuleCollider2D (solid), Rigidbody2D Dynamic gravity 0
//       child "Detection Radius"  CircleCollider2D m_IsTrigger: 1  <- 4.3, not today
//   Assets/_Scenes/Overworld.unity holds four MobileEnemy instances, one Coin.
//
// THE OLD PAGE TAUGHT A DIFFERENT FILE: a two-waypoint beat held in one
// `Transform target`, moving with `rb.linearVelocity = direction * moveSpeed`,
// on a prefab it claimed had "no collider and no body at all" (it has both).
// The build instead holds a LIST of any number of points, remembers an INDEX,
// and moves the Transform with Vector3.MoveTowards(). That is the MVP move —
// the same shortcut 1.4 took before 2.2 — and it is worth naming as debt, not
// hiding, because the body exists for other reasons (4.3's trigger, 4.6's hit).
//
// Scaffolding: GUIDED (level 2). The shape of the file is given and exactly one
// line is a gap: the wrap at the end of the list. That line carries the only
// idea in this lesson with no precedent anywhere before it — a route has an
// end, and the memory is a number that has to come back to the beginning. An
// index that reaches the end of a list is not a patrol, it is an exception.
//
// Answer-box ids are positional and persisted. The rewrite keeps the FIRST
// socratic block ("Designing a Beat") with its three questions in order, and
// the decompose block keeps both of its turns where they were.
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';
import { renderWalkthrough, renderAskBanner } from '../../lib/walkthrough';
import { renderDecompose } from '../../lib/decompose';

// The two recurring questions, applied to the beat. Level 2, so exactly one
// part is withheld — the one with no precedent in this course: not the memory
// itself (a number that changes is 1.3) but where the number has to go when it
// runs out. Its reading stays attached, because finding the answer in the docs
// is the skill being practised.
const DECOMPOSE = {
  big:
    'The enemy should walk a beat — back and forth, forever, with nobody at the keyboard. ' +
    '"Make the enemy patrol" is the whole feature, and at this moment the enemy is a picture ' +
    'with an empty script on it: it has never moved once.',
  parts: [
    {
      name: 'Write the route down as a list of places',
      does: 'One serialized `List<Transform>` slot on the prefab. Each entry is a Transform you place in the scene and drag in — a route of two points today, five later, without a line of new code.',
      doc: 'the `[SerializeField] private List<Transform>` row of this lesson\'s table, and the serialized-field rule from 1.3.',
      href: '1.3.html',
    },
    {
      name: 'Remember which point it is walking toward',
      does: 'One `int` — an index into the list, not a Transform. It starts at `0` and it is the only thing in the file that changes when the enemy turns around.',
      doc: 'the field declarations from 1.3, and the `int` you used for `WalkDir` in 1.6.',
      href: '1.3.html',
    },
    {
      name: 'Move toward that point at a constant speed',
      does: '`Vector3.MoveTowards()` with `speed * Time.deltaTime` as the step. It refuses to overshoot, so the enemy lands exactly on the point instead of sailing past it.',
      doc: 'the `Vector3.MoveTowards()` row of this lesson\'s table, and `Time.deltaTime` from 1.5.',
      href: '1.5.html',
    },
    {
      name: 'Arrive, advance, and wrap at the end of the list',
      doc: 'the `Vector3.Distance()` row in the quick reference, and `List<T>.Count` in the scripting reference.',
      href: '../docs/reference/unity-csharp-quickref.html',
      blank: true,
    },
  ],
  why:
    'Three of those four parts are work you have already done: a serialized field is 1.3, a number that changes ' +
    'is 1.3, and a distance compared against a threshold is 3.1. Only one part has no precedent at all: a route ' +
    'has an END. Every number this game has remembered so far was allowed to grow forever, and this one is not — ' +
    'the moment it equals the size of the list, reading the list throws.',
  sizeTest:
    'The test: could you look this part up on its own, right now, in one search? "Make an enemy patrol" ' +
    'cannot be looked up. "Move an object toward a position at a constant speed" can.',
  turns: [
    'The table prices three ways to define a route. Which did you choose, and what does your choice cost you — in your own words, not the table\'s?',
    'The enemy\'s whole memory is one `int`. What does it index, and what happens on the first frame it equals `patrolPoints.Count`?',
  ],
};

// Three code blocks, each opening with a comment saying where in EnemyController
// .cs it goes: the fields with the fields, the seed in Start(), the loop in
// Update(). One gap, on the wrap.
const WALK = {
  ask: 'how do I make an enemy follow a patrol route?',
  file: 'Assets/Scripts/EnemyController.cs',
  label: 'In the Code',
  intro: 'The enemy ships as a stub: an EnemyController on a prefab with a body, standing in the Overworld four times over. Today it gets a route, an index into that route, and the line that sends the index back to the start.',
  level: 2,
  steps: [
    {
      title: 'Declare the route and the index',
      lead: 'The `using` goes at the top of the file with the others; the rest goes in the class body, with your fields.',
      lines: [
        { code: '// EnemyController.cs — the top of the file, above the class.', what: '' },
        { code: 'using System.Collections.Generic;', what: 'The namespace `List<>` lives in. Unity adds `using UnityEngine;` for you when the script is created; this one you add yourself.' , why: '**Without it, `List` is an unknown name.** The compiler error points at the type — "The type or namespace name `List` could not be found" — which reads like the list is wrong when the only thing missing is one line at the top of the file.' },
        { code: '', what: '' },
        { code: '// EnemyController.cs — class body, above Start(), with your other fields.', what: '' },
        { code: '    [SerializeField] private List<Transform> patrolPoints = new List<Transform>();', what: '**The route.** A list of Transforms — each entry is an object you placed in the scene and dragged into the Inspector slot. `[SerializeField]` is what puts the slot there at all.' , why: '**`= new List<Transform>()` is not decoration.** Without it the field starts as `null`, and the first thing your code will do is ask it how many points it has — a NullReferenceException instead of an empty list. An empty list is a route with no points, which is a bug you can see; a null list is a crash that reads like a code fault. (Unity fills a serialized list for you once you press Play with one wired, which is exactly why the difference is invisible until it isn\'t.)' },
        { code: '    int patrolNumber = 0;', what: '**The memory.** Which entry in the list the enemy is walking toward right now. `0` is the first point; the line that moves it is the gap in step 3.' },
        { code: '    Vector3 targetPosition;', what: 'The spot in the world the enemy is walking to this frame — read out of the list so the movement line does not have to say `patrolPoints[patrolNumber].position` twice.' },
        { code: '', what: '' },
        { code: '    float speed = 2;', what: 'How fast it walks, in units **per second**.' },
      ],
    },
    {
      title: 'Seed the first destination',
      lead: '`Start()` runs once, before `Update()` has ever run.',
      lines: [
        { code: '// EnemyController.cs — inside Start().', what: '' },
        { code: '        targetPosition = patrolPoints[patrolNumber].position;', what: 'Reads entry `0` of the list and keeps its position. `Update()` will overwrite this every frame, so the line is really a declaration of intent: the enemy starts walking toward the first point.' , why: '**This line is where an empty list announces itself.** With nothing wired, `patrolPoints[0]` does not exist, and the Console fills with `ArgumentOutOfRangeException` before the first frame is drawn. The exception names this line, and the bug is in the Inspector.' },
      ],
    },
    {
      title: 'Arrive, advance, wrap, walk',
      lead: 'One `Update()` doing four things in order. Only one of them is new.',
      lines: [
        { code: '// EnemyController.cs — inside Update().', what: '' },
        { code: '        if (Vector3.Distance(transform.position, patrolPoints[patrolNumber].position) < .1f)', what: 'The arrival test: the distance check from 3.1, against a threshold of a tenth of a unit. True on every frame the enemy is standing close enough to the point it was walking toward.' },
        { code: '        {', what: '' },
        { code: '            patrolNumber += 1;', what: 'The turn: the index moves to the next point in the list. This is the whole reason the enemy can walk a beat with nobody at the keyboard — a player turns because a key was pressed, and the enemy has no keys.' },
        { code: '            ______________________________', blank: true, what: 'The wrap. The index has just been incremented, so it might now point past the end of the list — write the line that sends it back to the first point when it does.' , hint: 'Ask two questions in one condition: has the index reached the number of points in the list? If so, what should it be instead of that number? The list can tell you its own size.' , why: '**Leave this out and the enemy crashes at the end of its route.** The next line reads `patrolPoints[patrolNumber]`, and an index equal to `Count` is one past the last entry — `ArgumentOutOfRangeException`, every frame, forever. A list does not loop; the number that walks it has to. This is the difference between a route and a count.' },
        { code: '        }', what: '' },
        { code: '', what: '' },
        { code: '        targetPosition = patrolPoints[patrolNumber].position;', what: 'Re-read the destination from the list. On most frames this is the same point as last frame; on the frame the enemy arrived, it is the next one.' },
        { code: '', what: '' },
        { code: '        float step = speed * Time.deltaTime;', what: 'How far to travel **this frame**: speed is per second, so the frame\'s duration turns it into per frame — the multiplication from 1.5.' },
        { code: '        transform.position = Vector3.MoveTowards(transform.position, targetPosition, step);', what: 'Moves the object toward the destination by exactly one step, stopping on the point rather than passing it.' , why: '**`MoveTowards()` cannot overshoot.** A velocity line would travel `step` units in the direction of the point even when the point is only half a step away — the enemy would jitter past the waypoint and the arrival test would flicker. Because the third argument is already a distance, this line takes `speed * Time.deltaTime` rather than `speed` alone. **And it moves the Transform, not the body** — the same shortcut the Player took in 1.4 before 2.2 replaced it. That is a debt, not an accident: the body is still there, because 4.3 needs the trigger and 4.6 needs something to hit.' },
      ],
    },
  ],
  finalFile: `using UnityEngine;
using System.Collections.Generic;

public class EnemyController : MonoBehaviour
{
    float speed = 2;

    // The route: objects placed in the scene and dragged into the Inspector.
    [SerializeField] private List<Transform> patrolPoints = new List<Transform>();

    // The memory: which entry the enemy is walking toward.
    int patrolNumber = 0;

    Vector3 targetPosition;

    void Start()
    {
        targetPosition = patrolPoints[patrolNumber].position;
    }

    void Update()
    {
        if (Vector3.Distance(transform.position, patrolPoints[patrolNumber].position) < .1f)
        {
            patrolNumber += 1;

            // The wrap: a list does not loop, so the index has to.
            if (patrolNumber == patrolPoints.Count)
            {
                patrolNumber = 0;
            }
        }

        targetPosition = patrolPoints[patrolNumber].position;

        float step = speed * Time.deltaTime;
        transform.position = Vector3.MoveTowards(transform.position, targetPosition, step);
    }
}`,
  finalNote:
    'Compare against your own file rather than copying it. The gap is the wrap, and it is the one idea in this lesson with nothing earlier to copy. The file above writes it across three lines; a single-line `if (patrolNumber == patrolPoints.Count) patrolNumber = 0;` is the same program. Your waypoints, your speed and your threshold are yours; the shape is what has to match.',
};

const body = `<h1>4.1 — The Enemy: Patrol</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    The turret never moves. This enemy walks a beat with nobody at the keyboard — a route it can
    repeat forever, defined by objects you place in the scene.
</p>

${renderAskBanner('how do I make an enemy follow a patrol route?', 2)}

${renderDecompose('4.1', DECOMPOSE, 2)}

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity discovery">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">In the Editor</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~20 min</span>
    </div>

    <ol>
        <li>
            <strong>Hierarchy →</strong> count the <code>MobileEnemy</code> instances in the
            Overworld: <strong>four</strong>, against exactly one Coin. Write the count down before
            you move anything.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Project window →</strong> <code>Assets/Prefabs/MobileEnemy</code>, and select
            the prefab <em>asset</em>, not an instance in the scene. Its root is tagged
            <strong>Enemy</strong>, carries a <strong>Capsule Collider 2D</strong> and a
            <strong>Rigidbody 2D</strong> set to <strong>Dynamic</strong> with
            <strong>Gravity Scale 0</strong>. Read those: today's movement does not use the body,
            but 4.3 will, and you should know it is already there.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Hierarchy → MobileEnemy → Detection Radius</strong>: a child with a
            <strong>Circle Collider 2D</strong>, <strong>Is Trigger</strong> ticked. That is next
            lesson's aggro radius — leave it alone today, and notice that the project wired it before
            it wrote the script that uses it.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Hierarchy → right-click → Create Empty</strong>, twice, in the Overworld. Rename
            them <strong>PatrolPointA</strong> and <strong>PatrolPointB</strong>, and place them with
            the Move tool in the <strong>Scene view</strong> at the two ends of a beat that makes
            sense in the level. They are positions and nothing else.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Hierarchy →</strong> one enemy instance, <strong>Inspector →
            Enemy Controller → Patrol Points</strong>: set <strong>Size</strong> to
            <code>2</code>, then drag the two empty objects into the two slots. Repeat per instance
            if three enemies should walk three different routes.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Once the code half is done — Game view → press Play</strong> and watch one enemy
            walk the loop. Then add a third point to the list while Play is running and watch it take
            the longer route — nothing in the script changed.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Console →</strong> if you see <code>ArgumentOutOfRangeException</code>, the list
            is empty, or the index ran past its end. Check <strong>Size</strong> in the Inspector
            before you check the code.
        </li>
    </ol>

    <p>
        Read the three rows below before you write anything. All three are the same four parts in
        different clothes, and only what the field remembers differs — a place, an index, or a limit.
    </p>

    <table>
        <tr><th>The row</th><th>What it remembers, and how it turns</th><th>What it costs you</th></tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>A leash from the spawn point</strong></td>
            <td>The position it started at, plus how far it may wander. It measures the drift with <code>Vector3.Distance()</code> and flips the stored sign once that exceeds the leash.</td>
            <td>No scene setup at all, and the beat follows the enemy wherever you drag it. But it is always a straight line centred on the spawn point, and the turn is noticed only <em>after</em> the enemy has moved — so it always overshoots by up to one frame of travel.</td></tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>A list of placed waypoints</strong> — <em>this project</em></td>
            <td>A <code>List&lt;Transform&gt;</code> wired in the Inspector, plus an <code>int</code> remembering which entry it is heading for. It walks to that point, advances the index, and wraps to <code>0</code> at the end.</td>
            <td>You must place and wire every point, per instance, and guard the wrap — an index one past the end is an exception. In exchange the route is as long as you like without new code.</td></tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Two absolute bounds</strong></td>
            <td>Two numbers, a minimum and a maximum, and a sign the movement multiplies by.</td>
            <td>The simplest maths and the most brittle. Those bounds are world coordinates, so moving the enemy leaves its beat behind and a copied prefab turns on frame one. Let the sign ever reach <code>0</code> and the walk stops for good.</td></tr>
    </table>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic: Designing a Beat</h3>
        <ol>
<li>Which of the three route definitions fits an enemy you will place four or five times across a map and keep nudging while you design the level? What does your choice cost you?</li>
<li>The leash approach stores the position the enemy started at. Which lifecycle method must save it, and what would the enemy do if you saved it every frame instead?</li>
<li>The enemy's whole memory is one <code>int</code> that counts up. Why is an index enough to define a route — and what would go wrong with a bool such as <code>isGoingRight</code> once the route has five points?</li>
</ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
${renderWalkthrough(WALK)}

<!-- ============================================================ -->
